WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.625
"Grandpa! I got a starring role in the class play!" Laurie bounded into Grandpa's study.

2
00:00:11.626 --> 00:00:17.332
"That's terrific!" Grandpa said, giving her a hug. "Tell me more."

3
00:00:17.333 --> 00:00:26.214
"The title of the play is Medieval Nights , but our teacher says it's based on some book called The Canterbury

4
00:00:26.215 --> 00:00:31.528
Tales ." "Ah! That's one of the most famous books of the

5
00:00:31.529 --> 00:00:36.631
Middle Ages. It was written by a man named Geoffrey Chaucer,"

6
00:00:36.632 --> 00:00:41.276
Grandpa said. "The play is about a group of travelers going to visit

7
00:00:41.277 --> 00:00:47.646
a cathedral. I'm an English queen who meets a knight and sends him on a

8
00:00:47.647 --> 00:00:53.650
long journey!" Grandpa smiled. "Then I'm sure you live in a

9
00:00:53.651 --> 00:00:59.217
magnificent castle! How much do you know about cathedrals, knights, and castles?"

10
00:00:59.218 --> 00:01:06.310
he asked. "Not much," Laurie admitted. "My class is going to study the Middle Ages

11
00:01:06.311 --> 00:01:10.495
next week." "I could give you an introduction,"

12
00:01:10.496 --> 00:01:16.378
Grandpa offered. "Sure." Laurie settled down on

13
00:01:16.379 --> 00:01:25.374
the couch. "The Middle Ages refers to the time between the collapse of the Roman Empire and the beginning of the

14
00:01:25.375 --> 00:01:29.775
European Renaissance, so from around the fifth century to the

15
00:01:29.776 --> 00:01:35.908
fifteenth century. In Europe the Christian Church had become the richest, most

16
00:01:35.909 --> 00:01:43.223
powerful institution. You, Queen Laurie, might have been baptized in a cathedral, like the one in

17
00:01:43.224 --> 00:01:52.884
your play. You'd attend church every Sunday, get married in a church, pay taxes to the church, and go there to seek advice and

18
00:01:52.885 --> 00:01:57.685
make confessions. "Church buildings became really beautiful during the

19
00:01:57.686 --> 00:02:06.101
Middle Ages. Medieval architects designed tall buildings with pointy steeples that reached

20
00:02:06.102 --> 00:02:12.940
toward heaven. The insides were filled with expensive sculptures, golden goblets, and other

21
00:02:12.941 --> 00:02:18.630
fancy things. Huge, stained-glass windows flooded the church with

22
00:02:18.631 --> 00:02:26.524
colored light. But these beautiful windows weren't just decoration—they also

23
00:02:26.525 --> 00:02:33.419
told stories. Many people those days couldn't read, so how could they learn the stories of

24
00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:37.317
the Bible? Well, priests could tell them

25
00:02:37.318 --> 00:02:45.884
of course. But people could also look up at the massive windows and see the story of Jesus, for example, pictured in

26
00:02:45.885 --> 00:02:56.145
the glass." "Wow . . . Like a storybook." Laurie gazed at the windows in the study, trying to imagine a story

27
00:02:56.146 --> 00:03:01.737
painted there. "I bet there is a monk and a nun in your play too,"

28
00:03:01.738 --> 00:03:07.795
Grandpa said. "Yep. But I'm glad I get to be the queen and wear a nice costume,"

29
00:03:07.796 --> 00:03:12.563
Laurie confessed. "The nun just wears a boring gown and something to cover

30
00:03:12.564 --> 00:03:18.834
her head." "Men and women who became monks and nuns devoted their lives to

31
00:03:18.835 --> 00:03:23.710
the church. They didn't have time to worry about things like

32
00:03:23.711 --> 00:03:28.204
pretty clothes. They lived in their own communities, separated from

33
00:03:28.205 --> 00:03:36.349
the town. Monks wore plain, brown robes, ate simple food, and spent their days praying and studying

34
00:03:36.350 --> 00:03:40.300
the Bible. They were often well-educated and could read

35
00:03:40.301 --> 00:03:45.603
and write. Many copied out long sections of the Bible in

36
00:03:45.604 --> 00:03:54.811
beautiful script. Nuns devoted most of their day to prayer too, but they were busy with chores such as cooking, cleaning,

37
00:03:54.812 --> 00:04:00.360
and sewing. "Many medieval Christians wanted to create an entirely Christian

38
00:04:00.361 --> 00:04:04.650
world. But there were still strong empires with different

39
00:04:04.651 --> 00:04:11.602
religions. For example, the Muslim Abbasid Empire ruled northern Africa and the Middle

40
00:04:11.603 --> 00:04:21.186
East. European Christians didn't like that their holy land—including Jerusalem, where Christianity developed—was controlled by

41
00:04:21.187 --> 00:04:25.873
Muslims. Many Christians journeyed to these places to

42
00:04:25.874 --> 00:04:31.833
worship. And church leaders worried about Christians' safety because sometimes they were

43
00:04:31.834 --> 00:04:39.360
attacked. So the pope asked barons—wealthy landowners—to gather their armies and fight for Christianity.

44
00:04:39.361 --> 00:04:48.767
These battles, known as the Crusades, lasted for nearly two hundred years and killed almost nine million people.

45
00:04:48.768 --> 00:04:55.690
The Christian fighters were called Crusaders and wore red crosses on their armor.

46
00:04:55.691 --> 00:05:02.888
Many were knights. Is the knight in your play a Crusader, Laurie?" Grandpa asked.

47
00:05:02.889 --> 00:05:12.502
"I don't know! All I know is that he travels around and when he finally returns, he gets married and lives in a castle," Laurie said.

48
00:05:12.503 --> 00:05:17.887
"Is he chivalrous and handsome?" Grandpa asked, smiling.

49
00:05:17.888 --> 00:05:24.967
"Handsome? No! Yuck," Laurie answered, crinkling her nose.

50
00:05:24.968 --> 00:05:28.879
"And I don't know what chivalrous means."

51
00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:34.074
"Chivalrous means good, honorable, and polite to women.

52
00:05:34.075 --> 00:05:37.737
All knights were expected to be chivalrous.

53
00:05:37.738 --> 00:05:42.963
They practiced this, along with fighting skills, from a young age.

54
00:05:42.964 --> 00:05:53.137
Knights worked for barons. In exchange for protection, barons gave their knights some land and maybe a nice castle to live in.

55
00:05:53.138 --> 00:05:58.663
Battles were often deadly, so knights were respected for their bravery.

56
00:05:58.664 --> 00:06:04.379
Plus they made an awe-inspiring sight when they dressed for battle.

57
00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:11.453
In the early Middle Ages, a knight's armor was a suit made of tiny iron rings linked together.

58
00:06:11.454 --> 00:06:20.216
But eventually knights realized they needed more protection and began wearing suits made of iron and steel plates.

59
00:06:20.217 --> 00:06:26.782
It was like having a full-body shield that weighed over 22 kilograms!"

60
00:06:26.783 --> 00:06:33.860
"I'd rather hear about the castles," Laurie requested. "Since I'll be living in one."

61
00:06:33.861 --> 00:06:41.694
"Of course. Castles were large, sturdy stone buildings with many rooms.

62
00:06:41.695 --> 00:06:49.757
There were great candlelit halls for feasts and celebrations, but also dark, damp prisons.

63
00:06:49.758 --> 00:06:56.500
Castles had to protect the owner, as well as the servants, guards, and entertainers who lived there.

64
00:06:56.501 --> 00:07:00.824
Neighboring noblemen often fought each other for land.

65
00:07:00.825 --> 00:07:07.579
So many castles had stone defense walls that circled the entire castle.

66
00:07:07.580 --> 00:07:11.119
Sometimes there were even two or three walls.

67
00:07:11.120 --> 00:07:17.788
Guards would stand on top of the walls and shoot arrows or throw huge rocks at intruders.

68
00:07:17.789 --> 00:07:25.242
Many castles also had a moat, which was a man-made canal around the castle.

69
00:07:25.243 --> 00:07:31.884
In order to cross it, visitors needed to have a bridge let down, or else swim across.

70
00:07:31.885 --> 00:07:37.250
The tall towers were often lookout points to spot intruders.

71
00:07:37.251 --> 00:07:41.617
Or perhaps to spot a knight returning from his journey!"

72
00:07:41.618 --> 00:07:47.684
"Laurie! Will you help me make dinner?" Laurie's mom called from downstairs.

73
00:07:47.685 --> 00:07:53.638
"Coming, Mom!" Laurie hollered back. Then she giggled.

74
00:07:53.639 --> 00:08:02.261
"Silly Mom. Queens don't cook dinner!" She turned to Grandpa and raised her chin like a queen.

75
00:08:02.262 --> 00:08:07.426
"Be sure to join us for our feast in the great hall tonight."

76
00:08:07.427 --> 00:08:17.164
Bowing slightly, Grandpa answered, "It would be my pleasure, Your Highness."

